anubis source for Keel Linux
============================

Branches
--------

debian/sid   the Debian Go team's work-in-progress packaging, as on salsa
             (go-team/packages/anubis, ITP #1102132), unchanged
upstream     upstream imports; its history includes upstream's git history
keel/trixie  this package, built for the Keel repository

The layout is DEP-14's, with the tags upstream/<version> and
keel/<debian-version>. `gbp buildpackage` builds the three orig tarballs from
the upstream/<version> tag (there is no pristine-tar branch: the npm
component is assembled, not released), and `dpkg-buildpackage -b` from a
clone of keel/trixie works too, since the upstream branch carries
everything the build reads.

Upstream tarball and its two components
---------------------------------------

The main tarball is upstream's anubis-src-vendor-VERSION.tar.gz from the
GitHub release, which already contains vendor/ (go mod vendor), signed
with the key in debian/upstream/signing-key.asc:

  gpgv --keyring debian/upstream/signing-key.asc \
      anubis-src-vendor-VERSION.tar.gz.asc anubis-src-vendor-VERSION.tar.gz

Its tree is upstream's signed tag vVERSION plus vendor/ and doc/; gbp
import-orig --upstream-vcs-tag=vVERSION records the tag as the second
parent of the import commit.

For 1.28.2 (checked 2026-11-23):

  anubis-src-vendor-1.28.2.tar.gz
    sha256 f3cd582af433839947a2be596a74269ebe888697d96b675ed3fc184aa2edeef1
    good signature, key 833F64161167B501058C394756375DA2DF02ABFF
  tag v1.28.2 -> commit a1384fbf42e331652b6f5cc89fdbcdc255d25b8e,
    tag and commit signed with the same key; the tarball outside
    vendor/ and doc/ is that commit's tree, file for file
  anubis-src-vendor-npm-1.28.2.tar.gz (source of wasm-modules/)
    sha256 524faa5da63fe45ccc8f070cd8bb12c2ac98599ed4832a17b42c2d09c19c66c2
    good signature, same key

The sha256 values are also those that GitHub's release API gives for
the assets.

npm/ (the "npm" component of gbp.conf) holds the runtime JavaScript
dependencies that esbuild bundles into the challenge pages; nothing from
npm/ is installed as is. They are the closure of "preact",
"@aws-crypto/sha256-js" and "wasm-feature-detect" in package-lock.json:

  preact, @aws-crypto/sha256-js, @aws-crypto/util, @aws-sdk/types,
  @smithy/is-array-buffer, @smithy/types, @smithy/util-buffer-from,
  @smithy/util-utf8, tslib, wasm-feature-detect

To refresh it for a new upstream version, download each "resolved" URL of
package-lock.json, check it against its "integrity" (sha512, base64), and
unpack each tarball's package/ directory into npm/<name>. The development
dependencies (esbuild, postcss, cssnano, playwright, ...) are not needed:
esbuild comes from Debian and the CSS is bundled with esbuild. For 1.28.2
the sha256 of each npm tarball:

  5750638b4fb1e159e2a6eff84532d285511db49eeef2f698d6f8484d42e4a96a  @aws-crypto/sha256-js 5.2.0
  42aa9abd7adb5928b922e009433f46df0613a57f18bace8dbfb43cd1d0f772d3  @aws-crypto/util 5.2.0
  85c1c02cb4836e2911f9fbcc7c9e4f3fb14593f4442112aa1a455e7aa980bf82  @aws-sdk/types 3.840.0
  34f42607b0756d7ac1251cbb89f24a92a7cb1511c6a23975ddd91fea2d6ef7bd  @smithy/is-array-buffer 2.2.0
  03276b98544e9f9110d08a58bd8a7f5b6c01db1052ff64086d394294d8aa2c42  @smithy/types 4.3.1
  25141bd3368b188c51e4de6380f74143e11ae93006b90d661971b3684174b1f8  @smithy/util-buffer-from 2.2.0
  b137f56c3a32a49c883d0332e1c14bf413f5d7020395c650035b1f4ea2fa021a  @smithy/util-utf8 2.3.0
  b18cb0a457f3d43c7bb30391a74ade7d13e03bc6e77915e061c70c0fe1123299  preact 10.29.8
  66f635d5eeabae44807534976913a102cf615b9a045368359c9f79ae6ee2119e  tslib 2.8.1
  c56c60a94f74cde3fd08abc609fc9ef97683b501acd40c2bc7eaf5ad2632c5b3  wasm-feature-detect 1.9.0

wasm-modules/ (the "wasm-modules" component) holds the six WebAssembly
proof-of-work modules, baseline/ and simd128/ for argon2id, hashx and
sha256, copied unchanged from web/static/wasm/ of upstream's signed
anubis-src-vendor-npm-VERSION.tar.gz; wasm-modules/SHA256SUMS lists them
and debian/rules checks them against it. They are required at run time:
web/embed.go embeds web/static, and at start lib/config.go runs Setup for
every challenge method, which loads static/wasm/simd128/<method>.wasm (the
server validates those challenges by running the module with wazero).
Without them anubis does not start, even with a policy that uses none of
these methods.

Why upstream's build and not Keel's: building them from source with
trixie and trixie-backports does not work today.
- Rust for wasm32: trixie-backports has rustc and cargo 1.96.1 but
  libstd-rust-dev-wasm32 only 1.94.1 or 1.93.1, and the standard library
  must come from the same rustc. Trixie's own 1.85.1 matches, but the
  build-dependency resolution of the CI and of a plain backports build
  would then need pinning.
- The crates: hashx, equix and hybrid-array are not in Debian, and
  digest and sha2 are at 0.10 where Cargo.lock needs 0.11; a vendored
  crates component from crates.io would be needed.
- wasm-opt and wasm2js: wasm/scripts/build_wasm.sh re-encodes every
  module with wasm-opt for old browsers, and build_wasm2js.sh needs
  binaryen 130; trixie has binaryen 120, which upstream says is too old.
  Both scripts end at upstream's prebuilt utils/wasm/wasm2js/*_130.wasm
  in any case.
So the modules would be built from upstream's sources with an upstream
prebuilt tool either way. The modules from the signed tarball are used
instead, and the change is one step: when Debian has a matching Rust for
wasm32 and binaryen 130, wasm-modules/ can go and debian/rules can run
build_wasm.sh.

The other generated files are built here: web/js/gen/wasm2js/*.wasm.js
by wasm/scripts/build_wasm2js.sh from the baseline modules, with
upstream's binaryen 130 build in the source (utils/wasm/wasm2js/
wasm2js_130.wasm, Apache-2.0) run by the vendored wazero
(utils/cmd/wazero-exec); and all JavaScript and CSS with esbuild.

Licences of the modules (debian/copyright): hashx.wasm contains hashx
and equix, LGPL-3.0-only; every module contains wee_alloc and
memory_units, MPL-2.0. The sources of those crates are not in this
source package (debian/TODO.Debian).

Generated files
---------------

The build regenerates the templ and stringer output with the vendored
tools (go generate) and builds all JavaScript and CSS from source. The
precompressed .gz, .zst and .br copies that web/build.sh writes are
embedded but never served (anubis compresses on the fly).

Towards Debian
--------------

Debian's Go team packages each module separately instead of vendoring;
see debian/TODO.Debian for what remains before this can converge with
the ITP.
